{
  "findings": [
    {
      "severity": "concern",
      "claim": "The spec declares no `risk:` field (defaults to `standard`, `declared_risk` is `None`), even though `saffron/cell/worktree.py` is in `touches` and matches `policy.yaml`'s `elevate_on` entry `saffron/cell/**`, which will auto-elevate the diff to `elevated` at build time per DESIGN.md regardless of the frontmatter. This is harmless for the `size` gate itself (the measured 457-token diff is far under the 4200-token `refactor` ceiling either way), but `tasks.risk` (the ledger's pre-declared tier) will read `standard` for this task, unlike sibling spec `SA-0400` in the same batch, which touches the same `saffron/cell/**` tree and does declare `risk: elevated` explicitly. An operator should decide whether `SA-0399` should likewise declare `risk: elevated` for consistent queue treatment and visibility, even though nothing here would cause a wrong build.",
      "criterion": null,
      "file": null,
      "line": null,
      "fixes": null
    },
    {
      "severity": "note",
      "claim": "The \"Notes for the agent\" section states that criteria 1 and 2 \"declare no mutant and witness reports skip for them,\" but `saffron/gates/core/witness.py`'s own docstring describes `skip` as a single verdict over the whole criteria worklist (declared-plus-derived mutants across all criteria in the spec), not a per-criterion result. Since none of this spec's three criteria carries an explicit `mutant:` field, whether the gate's overall status is `skip` depends on whether criterion 3 (the `preserves` one) picks up a `derived` mutant from the repo's existing `witness:` patterns, which this spec doesn't address either way. This doesn't change anything the cell should build and costs nothing to leave for the operator, but the phrasing is slightly imprecise about the gate's actual granularity.",
      "criterion": null,
